xnu 0371: read the regrown trees, drop the reserve, and arm the app probe

Three measurements on 2026-11-19 shape this patch.

`statfs` answered f_bfree 41946 blocks (164 MB) with f_bavail 3 (12 KB): the
root mount withholds one percent of the volume as a free-block reserve, so every
writer that honours the available count refuses. That is what PhotoKit reads, and
it is why an import answers `PHPhotosErrorDomain 3305 (NotEnoughSpace)` on a
volume with 164 MB free. The reserve protects a system with space to spare this
volume does not have, so it is dropped for this mount.

The trees a boot regrows are also invisible: `/private/var/log` answers a readdir
with only "." and ".." while `unlink` of a file below it succeeds, because the
directory's catalog valence is zero and `cat_getdirentries` takes it as the entry
limit. `ls PATH` therefore bumps the valence in memory while it walks, and prints
each entry with its size.

`del PATH` unlinks one path in the kernel context and answers the errno.
`deltree PATH` removes every file under one of the two allowed trees
(`/private/var/log`, `/private/var/db/diagnostics`) and leaves the skeleton: an
empty directory holds no data blocks, so the space is the files. Root is the only
context that can read them -- the mobile account gets EACCES on `/private/var/db`.
Both print the mount's free and available blocks, which is how the reserve change
is measured.

The probe compared the exec name with the whole string, so an app that execs as
`/Applications/MobileSlideShow.app/MobileSlideShow` never matched: SpringBoard
and the poster extensions arrive as bare names, the app does not. It now compares
the last path component, and prints the names that miss (twelve a boot) until the
list is proven.

